\description{
Stacked bars of relative abundance, one per sample, drawn so that they
can be read: the samples ordered by their compositions (neighbouring bars
are similar), the most abundant features on the common baseline, and,
with \code{feature_groups}, one hue per higher-level group (such as a phylum)
with shades for its most abundant members (microshades' idea; Dahl et al.
2022). An optional heatmap of centred log ratios below the bars gives
every drawn feature its own aligned row.
}
\details{
The bars show \strong{proportions only}: each sample closed to 1. They are not
a compositional analysis, and they say nothing about how much of anything
a sample held. For absolute loads, scale the counts with \code{\link[=qmp_scale]{qmp_scale()}}
and draw them with \code{\link[graphics:barplot]{graphics::barplot()}}; for compositional readings of
the same samples, see the heatmap (\code{heatmap = TRUE}), \code{\link[=aitchison]{aitchison()}}
distances with \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, the covariance biplot of \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} with \code{norm = "clr"} (\code{plot(..., scaling = "covariance")}), \code{\link[=ternary_plot]{ternary_plot()}} for three
parts at a time, and the planned \code{variation_array()}.

\strong{Which features.} Each sample's counts are divided by their sum, and
the features are ranked by their mean proportion over the samples (ties
in their order in \code{counts}).
\itemize{
\item Without \code{feature_groups}: the \code{n_features} most abundant features are
drawn, and the rest summed into \code{"Other"}.
\item With \code{feature_groups}: the \code{n_feature_groups} groups with the largest
mean proportion (the sum of their members') are drawn; within each,
its \code{n_features} most abundant members, and the rest of the group as
\code{"Other <group>"}. The remaining groups are pooled as \code{"Other"}.
microshades ranks by the sum over samples instead, which ranks the
same.
}

Features whose proportion is zero in every sample are never drawn, and
segments that would hold nothing are left out.

\strong{Stack order}, from the bottom: the drawn groups from most to least
abundant, within each its members from most to least abundant and then
\code{"Other <group>"}; \code{"Other"} on top. Without groups, the features from
most to least abundant, then \code{"Other"}. The legend lists the segments
from the top down in this order (the most abundant first), each group's
under its name in bold.

\strong{Colours.} Group \eqn{g} of the \eqn{G} drawn has hue \eqn{h_g = 15 +
360 (g - 1) / G}; its \eqn{k} drawn members are shaded
\code{hcl(h_g, c = 55, l = seq(35, 80, length.out = k + 1))}, darkest for
the most abundant, and its \code{"Other <group>"} takes the lightest. Without
groups, the features take \code{\link[grDevices:hcl.colors]{grDevices::hcl.colors()}}'s \code{"Dark 3"}
palette. \code{"Other"} is \code{"grey80"}. These follow microshades' scheme (one
hue per group, shades from dark to light, the group's "Other" lightest),
re-derived in HCL rather than copied. Distinct hues with a luminance ramp
are reasonably robust to colour-vision deficiencies; for strict needs,
give \code{col}.

\strong{Sample order.} By \code{clust}, from the samples' \code{\link[=aitchison]{aitchison()}} distances
(with \code{pseudocount}), as \code{\link[=beta_div]{beta_div()}} orders them (\code{clust = "pcoa"} is
\code{order(pcoa(aitchison(counts))[, 1])}). With \code{groups}, the samples are
then sorted by group, keeping that order within each group. The
subtitle says how the samples are ordered, and how much of the total
\code{"Other"} holds (its mean proportion over the samples).

\strong{Heatmap.} With \code{heatmap = TRUE}, the drawn features' centred log
