\section{Why stacked bars mislead, and what this one does}{

No peer-reviewed paper critiques microbiome stacked bars on their own:
the critiques below come from the motivations of tool papers, and from
tutorials and forum threads.\tabular{ll}{
   Critique (source) \tab How this plot answers it \cr
   Segments' lengths are hard to compare across bars (Peeters et al. 2024) \tab Samples are ordered by their compositions, so neighbouring bars are similar and gradients read as gradients; the most abundant feature sits on the common baseline; \code{heatmap = TRUE} gives every feature its own aligned row \cr
   "Other" hides rare taxa, and many taxa make colour hard (Dahl et al. 2022; Peeters et al. 2024) \tab Hierarchical shading: one hue per group, shades for its top members, the group's own "Other" lightest; the subtitle says how much "Other" holds \cr
   Averaging samples into one bar per group distorts (tutorials and forum threads, not peer reviewed) \tab Never averaged: one bar per sample, and groups are panels \cr
   Closure hides absolute load (Gloor et al. 2017) \tab The bars are proportions only; for loads, use \code{\link[=qmp_scale]{qmp_scale()}} and a plain \code{\link[graphics:barplot]{graphics::barplot()}} \cr
   Stacked bars ignore phylogeny and differential abundance (Cuisiniere & Santos 2024) \tab Out of scope: see \code{\link[=plot.ecodive_ord]{plot.ecodive_ord()}} and the plots of \code{\link[=da_test]{da_test()}}'s results \cr
}
